{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/agent-source && cat -n config.go'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// Copyright IBM Corp. 2013, 2026\n     2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0\n     3\t\n     4\tpackage raft\n     5\t\n     6\timport (\n     7\t\t\"fmt\"\n     8\t\t\"io\"\n     9\t\t\"os\"\n    10\t\t\"time\"\n    11\t\n    12\t\t\"github.com/hashicorp/go-hclog\"\n    13\t)\n    14\t\n    15\t// ProtocolVersion is the version of the protocol (which includes RPC messages\n    16\t// as well as Raft-specific log entries) that this server can _understand_. Use\n    17\t// the ProtocolVersion member of the Config object to control the version of\n    18\t// the protocol to use when _speaking_ to other servers. Note that depending on\n    19\t// the protocol version being spoken, some otherwise understood RPC messages\n    20\t// may be refused. See dispositionRPC for details of this logic.\n    21\t//\n    22\t// There are notes about the upgrade path in the description of the versions\n    23\t// below. If you are starting a fresh cluster then there's no reason not to\n    24\t// jump right to the latest protocol version. If you need to interoperate with\n    25\t// older, version 0 Raft servers you'll need to drive the cluster through the\n    26\t// different versions in order.\n    27\t//\n    28\t// The version details are complicated, but here's a summary of what's required\n    29\t// to get from a version 0 cluster to version 3:\n    30\t//\n    31\t//  1. In version N of your app that starts using the new Raft library with\n    32\t//     versioning, set ProtocolVersion to 1.\n    33\t//  2. Make version N+1 of your app require version N as a prerequisite (all\n    34\t//     servers must be upgraded). For version N+1 of your app set ProtocolVersion\n    35\t//     to 2.\n    36\t//  3. Similarly, make version N+2 of your app require version N+1 as a\n    37\t//     prerequisite. For version N+2 of your app, set ProtocolVersion to 3.\n    38\t//\n    39\t// During this upgrade, older cluster members will still have Server IDs equal\n    40\t// to their network addresses. To upgrade an older member and give it an ID, it\n    41\t// needs to leave the cluster and re-enter:\n    42\t//\n    43\t//  1. Remove the server from the cluster with RemoveServer, using its network\n    44\t//     address as its ServerID.\n    45\t//  2. Update the server's config to use a UUID or something else that is\n    46\t//     not tied to the machine as the ServerID (restarting the server).\n    47\t//  3. Add the server back to the cluster with AddVoter, using its new ID.\n    48\t//\n    49\t// You can do this during the rolling upgrade from N+1 to N+2 of your app, or\n    50\t// as a rolling change at any time after the upgrade.\n    51\t//\n    52\t// # Version History\n    53\t//\n    54\t// 0: Original Raft library before versioning was added. Servers running this\n    55\t//\n    56\t//\tversion of the Raft library use AddPeerDeprecated/RemovePeerDeprecated\n    57\t//\tfor all configuration changes, and have no support for LogConfiguration.\n    58\t//\n    59\t// 1: First versioned protocol, used to interoperate with old servers, and begin\n    60\t//\n    61\t//\tthe migration path to newer versions of the protocol. Under this version\n    62\t//\tall configuration changes are propagated using the now-deprecated\n    63\t//\tRemovePeerDeprecated Raft log entry. This means that server IDs are always\n    64\t//\tset to be the same as the server addresses (since the old log entry type\n    65\t//\tcannot transmit an ID), and only AddPeer/RemovePeer APIs are supported.\n    66\t//\tServers running this version of the protocol can understand the new\n    67\t//\tLogConfiguration Raft log entry but will never generate one so they can\n    68\t//\tremain compatible with version 0 Raft servers in the cluster.\n    69\t//\n    70\t// 2: Transitional protocol used when migrating an existing cluster to the new\n    71\t//\n    72\t//\tserver ID system. Server IDs are still set to be the same as server\n    73\t//\taddresses, but all configuration changes are propagated using the new\n    74\t//\tLogConfiguration Raft log entry type, which can carry full ID information.\n    75\t//\tThis version supports the old AddPeer/RemovePeer APIs as well as the new\n    76\t//\tID-based AddVoter/RemoveServer APIs which should be used when adding\n    77\t//\tversion 3 servers to the cluster later. This version sheds all\n    78\t//\tinteroperability with version 0 servers, but can interoperate with newer\n    79\t//\tRaft servers running with protocol version 1 since they can understand the\n    80\t//\tnew LogConfiguration Raft log entry, and this version can still understand\n    81\t//\ttheir RemovePeerDeprecated Raft log entries. We need this protocol version\n    82\t//\tas an intermediate step between 1 and 3 so that servers will propagate the\n    83\t//\tID information that will come from newly-added (or -rolled) servers using\n    84\t//\tprotocol version 3, but since they are still using their address-based IDs\n    85\t//\tfrom the previous step they will still be able to track commitments and\n    86\t//\ttheir own voting status properly. If we skipped this step, servers would\n    87\t//\tbe started with their new IDs, but they wouldn't see themselves in the old\n    88\t//\taddress-based configuration, so none of the servers would think they had a\n    89\t//\tvote.\n    90\t//\n    91\t// 3: Protocol adding full support for server IDs and new ID-based server APIs\n    92\t//\n    93\t//\t(AddVoter, AddNonvoter, etc.), old AddPeer/RemovePeer APIs are no longer\n    94\t//\tsupported. Version 2 servers should be swapped out by removing them from\n    95\t//\tthe cluster one-by-one and re-adding them with updated configuration for\n    96\t//\tthis protocol version, along with their server ID. The remove/add cycle\n    97\t//\tis required to populate their server ID. Note that removing must be done\n    98\t//\tby ID, which will be the old server's address.\n    99\ttype ProtocolVersion int\n   100\t\n   101\tconst (\n   102\t\t// ProtocolVersionMin is the minimum protocol version\n   103\t\tProtocolVersionMin ProtocolVersion = 0\n   104\t\t// ProtocolVersionMax is the maximum protocol version\n   105\t\tProtocolVersionMax = 3\n   106\t)\n   107\t\n   108\t// SnapshotVersion is the version of snapshots that this server can understand.\n   109\t// Currently, it is always assumed that the server generates the latest version,\n   110\t// though this may be changed in the future to include a configurable version.\n   111\t//\n   112\t// # Version History\n   113\t//\n   114\t// 0: Original Raft library before versioning was added. The peers portion of\n   115\t//\n   116\t//\tthese snapshots is encoded in the legacy format which requires decodePeers\n   117\t//\tto parse. This version of snapshots should only be produced by the\n   118\t//\tunversioned Raft library.\n   119\t//\n   120\t// 1: New format which adds support for a full configuration structure and its\n   121\t//\n   122\t//\tassociated log index, with support for server IDs and non-voting server\n   123\t//\tmodes. To ease upgrades, this also includes the legacy peers structure but\n   124\t//\tthat will never be used by servers that understand version 1 snapshots.\n   125\t//\tSince the original Raft library didn't enforce any versioning, we must\n   126\t//\tinclude the legacy peers structure for this version, but we can deprecate\n   127\t//\tit in the next snapshot version.\n   128\ttype SnapshotVersion int\n   129\t\n   130\tconst (\n   131\t\t// SnapshotVersionMin is the minimum snapshot version\n   132\t\tSnapshotVersionMin SnapshotVersion = 0\n   133\t\t// SnapshotVersionMax is the maximum snapshot version\n   134\t\tSnapshotVersionMax = 1\n   135\t)\n   136\t\n   137\t// Config provides any necessary configuration for the Raft server.\n   138\ttype Config struct {\n   139\t\t// ProtocolVersion allows a Raft server to inter-operate with older\n   140\t\t// Raft servers running an older version of the code. This is used to\n   141\t\t// version the wire protocol as well as Raft-specific log entries that\n   142\t\t// the server uses when _speaking_ to other servers. There is currently\n   143\t\t// no auto-negotiation of versions so all servers must be manually\n   144\t\t// configured with compatible versions. See ProtocolVersionMin and\n   145\t\t// ProtocolVersionMax for the versions of the protocol that this server\n   146\t\t// can _understand_.\n   147\t\tProtocolVersion ProtocolVersion\n   148\t\n   149\t\t// HeartbeatTimeout specifies the time in follower state without contact\n   150\t\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n   151\t\tHeartbeatTimeout time.Duration\n   152\t\n   153\t\t// ElectionTimeout specifies the time in candidate state without contact\n   154\t\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n   155\t\tElectionTimeout time.Duration\n   156\t\n   157\t\t// CommitTimeout specifies the time without an Apply operation before the\n   158\t\t// leader sends an AppendEntry RPC to followers, to ensure a timely commit of\n   159\t\t// log entries.\n   160\t\t// Due to random staggering, may be delayed as much as 2x this value.\n   161\t\tCommitTimeout time.Duration\n   162\t\n   163\t\t// MaxAppendEntries controls the maximum number of append entries\n   164\t\t// to send at once. We want to strike a balance between efficiency\n   165\t\t// and avoiding waste if the follower is going to reject because of\n   166\t\t// an inconsistent log.\n   167\t\tMaxAppendEntries int\n   168\t\n   169\t\t// BatchApplyCh indicates whether we should buffer applyCh\n   170\t\t// to size MaxAppendEntries. This enables batch log commitment,\n   171\t\t// but breaks the timeout guarantee on Apply. Specifically,\n   172\t\t// a log can be added to the applyCh buffer but not actually be\n   173\t\t// processed until after the specified timeout.\n   174\t\tBatchApplyCh bool\n   175\t\n   176\t\t// If we are a member of a cluster, and RemovePeer is invoked for the\n   177\t\t// local node, then we forget all peers and transition into the follower state.\n   178\t\t// If ShutdownOnRemove is set, we additional shutdown Raft. Otherwise,\n   179\t\t// we can become a leader of a cluster containing only this node.\n   180\t\tShutdownOnRemove bool\n   181\t\n   182\t\t// TrailingLogs controls how many logs we leave after a snapshot. This is used\n   183\t\t// so that we can quickly replay logs on a follower instead of being forced to\n   184\t\t// send an entire snapshot. The value passed here is the initial setting used.\n   185\t\t// This can be tuned during operation using ReloadConfig.\n   186\t\tTrailingLogs uint64\n   187\t\n   188\t\t// SnapshotInterval controls how often we check if we should perform a\n   189\t\t// snapshot. We randomly stagger between this value and 2x this value to avoid\n   190\t\t// the entire cluster from performing a snapshot at once. The value passed\n   191\t\t// here is the initial setting used. This can be tuned during operation using\n   192\t\t// ReloadConfig.\n   193\t\tSnapshotInterval time.Duration\n   194\t\n   195\t\t// SnapshotThreshold controls how many outstanding logs there must be before\n   196\t\t// we perform a snapshot. This is to prevent excessive snapshotting by\n   197\t\t// replaying a small set of logs instead. The value passed here is the initial\n   198\t\t// setting used. This can be tuned during operation using ReloadConfig.\n   199\t\tSnapshotThreshold uint64\n   200\t\n   201\t\t// LeaderLeaseTimeout is used to control how long the \"lease\" lasts\n   202\t\t// for being the leader without being able to contact a quorum\n   203\t\t// of nodes. If we reach this interval without contact, we will\n   204\t\t// step down as leader.\n   205\t\tLeaderLeaseTimeout time.Duration\n   206\t\n   207\t\t// LocalID is a unique ID for this server across all time. When running with\n   208\t\t// ProtocolVersion < 3, you must set this to be the same as the network\n   209\t\t// address of your transport.\n   210\t\tLocalID ServerID\n   211\t\n   212\t\t// NotifyCh is used to provide a channel that will be notified of leadership\n   213\t\t// changes. Raft will block writing to this channel, so it should either be\n   214\t\t// buffered or aggressively consumed.\n   215\t\tNotifyCh chan<- bool\n   216\t\n   217\t\t// LogOutput is used as a sink for logs, unless Logger is specified.\n   218\t\t// Defaults to os.Stderr.\n   219\t\tLogOutput io.Writer\n   220\t\n   221\t\t// LogLevel represents a log level. If the value does not match a known\n   222\t\t// logging level hclog.NoLevel is used.\n   223\t\tLogLevel string\n   224\t\n   225\t\t// Logger is a user-provided logger. If nil, a logger writing to\n   226\t\t// LogOutput with LogLevel is used.\n   227\t\tLogger hclog.Logger\n   228\t\n   229\t\t// NoSnapshotRestoreOnStart controls if raft will restore a snapshot to the\n   230\t\t// FSM on start. This is useful if your FSM recovers from other mechanisms\n   231\t\t// than raft snapshotting. Snapshot metadata will still be used to initialize\n   232\t\t// raft's configuration and index values.\n   233\t\tNoSnapshotRestoreOnStart bool\n   234\t\n   235\t\t// PreVoteDisabled deactivate the pre-vote feature when set to true\n   236\t\tPreVoteDisabled bool\n   237\t\n   238\t\t// NoLegacyTelemetry allows to skip the legacy metrics to avoid duplicates.\n   239\t\t// legacy metrics are those that have `_peer_name` as metric suffix instead as labels.\n   240\t\t// e.g: raft_replication_heartbeat_peer0\n   241\t\tNoLegacyTelemetry bool\n   242\t\n   243\t\t// RestoreCommittedLogs controls if the Raft server should use the restore committed logs\n   244\t\t// mechanism. Restore committed logs requires a LogStore implementation that\n   245\t\t// support commit tracking. When such a store is used and this config\n   246\t\t// enabled, raft nodes will replay all known-committed logs on disk\n   247\t\t// before completing `NewRaft` on startup. This is mainly useful where\n   248\t\t// the application allows relaxed-consistency reads from followers as it\n   249\t\t// will reduce how far behind the follower's FSM is when it starts. If all reads\n   250\t\t// are forwarded to the leader then there won't be observable benefit from this feature.\n   251\t\t//\n   252\t\t// Notice: If this is enabled, the log store MUST implement the CommitTrackingLogStore\n   253\t\t// interface. Otherwise, Raft will fail to start and return ErrIncompatibleLogStore.\n   254\t\tRestoreCommittedLogs bool\n   255\t\n   256\t\t// skipStartup allows NewRaft() to bypass all background work goroutines\n   257\t\tskipStartup bool\n   258\t}\n   259\t\n   260\tfunc (conf *Config) getOrCreateLogger() hclog.Logger {\n   261\t\tif conf.Logger != nil {\n   262\t\t\treturn conf.Logger\n   263\t\t}\n   264\t\tif conf.LogOutput == nil {\n   265\t\t\tconf.LogOutput = os.Stderr\n   266\t\t}\n   267\t\n   268\t\treturn hclog.New(&hclog.LoggerOptions{\n   269\t\t\tName:   \"raft\",\n   270\t\t\tLevel:  hclog.LevelFromString(conf.LogLevel),\n   271\t\t\tOutput: conf.LogOutput,\n   272\t\t})\n   273\t}\n   274\t\n   275\t// ReloadableConfig is the subset of Config that may be reconfigured during\n   276\t// runtime using raft.ReloadConfig. We choose to duplicate fields over embedding\n   277\t// or accepting a Config but only using specific fields to keep the API clear.\n   278\t// Reconfiguring some fields is potentially dangerous so we should only\n   279\t// selectively enable it for fields where that is allowed.\n   280\ttype ReloadableConfig struct {\n   281\t\t// TrailingLogs controls how many logs we leave after a snapshot. This is used\n   282\t\t// so that we can quickly replay logs on a follower instead of being forced to\n   283\t\t// send an entire snapshot. The value passed here updates the setting at runtime\n   284\t\t// which will take effect as soon as the next snapshot completes and truncation\n   285\t\t// occurs.\n   286\t\tTrailingLogs uint64\n   287\t\n   288\t\t// SnapshotInterval controls how often we check if we should perform a snapshot.\n   289\t\t// We randomly stagger between this value and 2x this value to avoid the entire\n   290\t\t// cluster from performing a snapshot at once.\n   291\t\tSnapshotInterval time.Duration\n   292\t\n   293\t\t// SnapshotThreshold controls how many outstanding logs there must be before\n   294\t\t// we perform a snapshot. This is to prevent excessive snapshots when we can\n   295\t\t// just replay a small set of logs.\n   296\t\tSnapshotThreshold uint64\n   297\t\n   298\t\t// HeartbeatTimeout specifies the time in follower state without\n   299\t\t// a leader before we attempt an election.\n   300\t\tHeartbeatTimeout time.Duration\n   301\t\n   302\t\t// ElectionTimeout specifies the time in candidate state without\n   303\t\t// a leader before we attempt an election.\n   304\t\tElectionTimeout time.Duration\n   305\t}\n   306\t\n   307\t// apply sets the reloadable fields on the passed Config to the values in\n   308\t// `ReloadableConfig`. It returns a copy of Config with the fields from this\n   309\t// ReloadableConfig set.\n   310\tfunc (rc *ReloadableConfig) apply(to Config) Config {\n   311\t\tto.TrailingLogs = rc.TrailingLogs\n   312\t\tto.SnapshotInterval = rc.SnapshotInterval\n   313\t\tto.SnapshotThreshold = rc.SnapshotThreshold\n   314\t\tto.HeartbeatTimeout = rc.HeartbeatTimeout\n   315\t\tto.ElectionTimeout = rc.ElectionTimeout\n   316\t\treturn to\n   317\t}\n   318\t\n   319\t// fromConfig copies the reloadable fields from the passed Config.\n   320\tfunc (rc *ReloadableConfig) fromConfig(from Config) {\n   321\t\trc.TrailingLogs = from.TrailingLogs\n   322\t\trc.SnapshotInterval = from.SnapshotInterval\n   323\t\trc.SnapshotThreshold = from.SnapshotThreshold\n   324\t\trc.HeartbeatTimeout = from.HeartbeatTimeout\n   325\t\trc.ElectionTimeout = from.ElectionTimeout\n   326\t}\n   327\t\n   328\t// DefaultConfig returns a Config with usable defaults.\n   329\tfunc DefaultConfig() *Config {\n   330\t\treturn &Config{\n   331\t\t\tProtocolVersion:    ProtocolVersionMax,\n   332\t\t\tHeartbeatTimeout:   1000 * time.Millisecond,\n   333\t\t\tElectionTimeout:    1000 * time.Millisecond,\n   334\t\t\tCommitTimeout:      50 * time.Millisecond,\n   335\t\t\tMaxAppendEntries:   64,\n   336\t\t\tShutdownOnRemove:   true,\n   337\t\t\tTrailingLogs:       10240,\n   338\t\t\tSnapshotInterval:   120 * time.Second,\n   339\t\t\tSnapshotThreshold:  8192,\n   340\t\t\tLeaderLeaseTimeout: 500 * time.Millisecond,\n   341\t\t\tLogLevel:           \"DEBUG\",\n   342\t\t}\n   343\t}\n   344\t\n   345\t// ValidateConfig is used to validate a sane configuration\n   346\tfunc ValidateConfig(config *Config) error {\n   347\t\t// We don't actually support running as 0 in the library any more, but\n   348\t\t// we do understand it.\n   349\t\tprotocolMin := ProtocolVersionMin\n   350\t\tif protocolMin == 0 {\n   351\t\t\tprotocolMin = 1\n   352\t\t}\n   353\t\tif config.ProtocolVersion < protocolMin ||\n   354\t\t\tconfig.ProtocolVersion > ProtocolVersionMax {\n   355\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"ProtocolVersion %d must be >= %d and <= %d\",\n   356\t\t\t\tconfig.ProtocolVersion, protocolMin, ProtocolVersionMax)\n   357\t\t}\n   358\t\tif len(config.LocalID) == 0 {\n   359\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"LocalID cannot be empty\")\n   360\t\t}\n   361\t\tif config.HeartbeatTimeout < 5*time.Millisecond {\n   362\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"HeartbeatTimeout is too low\")\n   363\t\t}\n   364\t\tif config.ElectionTimeout < 5*time.Millisecond {\n   365\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"ElectionTimeout is too low\")\n   366\t\t}\n   367\t\tif config.CommitTimeout < time.Millisecond {\n   368\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"CommitTimeout is too low\")\n   369\t\t}\n   370\t\tif config.MaxAppendEntries <= 0 {\n   371\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"MaxAppendEntries must be positive\")\n   372\t\t}\n   373\t\tif config.MaxAppendEntries > 1024 {\n   374\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"MaxAppendEntries is too large\")\n   375\t\t}\n   376\t\tif config.SnapshotInterval < 5*time.Millisecond {\n   377\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"SnapshotInterval is too low\")\n   378\t\t}\n   379\t\tif config.LeaderLeaseTimeout < 5*time.Millisecond {\n   380\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"LeaderLeaseTimeout is too low\")\n   381\t\t}\n   382\t\tif config.LeaderLeaseTimeout > config.HeartbeatTimeout {\n   383\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"LeaderLeaseTimeout (%s) cannot be larger than heartbeat timeout (%s)\", config.LeaderLeaseTimeout, config.HeartbeatTimeout)\n   384\t\t}\n   385\t\tif config.ElectionTimeout < config.HeartbeatTimeout {\n   386\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"ElectionTimeout (%s) must be equal or greater than Heartbeat Timeout (%s)\", config.ElectionTimeout, config.HeartbeatTimeout)\n   387\t\t}\n   388\t\treturn nil\n   389\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
